{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והמלאכה", :translit "ve-hamelakhah", :gloss "and the work"} {:he "והמלאכה היתה דים", :translit "ve-hamelakhah haytah dayim", :gloss "and the work was sufficient"} {:he "והמלאכה לא ליום אחד ולא לשנים", :translit "ve-hamelakhah lo le-yom echad ve-lo le-shnayim", :gloss "and the work not for one day and not for two"} {:he "בעזרא", :translit "be-ezra", :gloss "in Ezra"} {:he "והמלאכה גדולה", :translit "ve-hamelakhah gedolah", :gloss "and the work was great"} {:he "בד\"ה", :translit "be-divrei ha-yamim", :gloss "in Chronicles"} {:he "בענין בנין הבית", :translit "be-inyan binyan ha-bayit", :gloss "in the matter of building the House"} {:he "היתה דים", :translit "haytah dayim", :gloss "was sufficient"} {:he "נשתלחה ברכה במעשה ידיהם", :translit "nishtelachah berakhah be-ma'aseh yadeihem", :gloss "a blessing was sent in the work of their hands"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["והמלאכה היתה דים" "והמלאכה לא ליום אחד ולשנים" "והמלאכה גדולה"]} {:kind :other, :note "The three occurrences span Exodus, Ezra, and Chronicles, all in contexts of building the House of God"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'והמלאכה היתה דים' appears in the verse (Exodus 36:7) describing how the Tabernacle materials were sufficient. The Masorah notes the phrase occurs three times, including in Ezra (describing the Temple construction, Ezra 5:11 'והמלאכה לא ליום אחד ולא לשנים') and in Chronicles ('והמלאכה גדולה', 2 Chronicles 24:13). The concept that a blessing rests in sacred construction work draws from the biblical theme that God's house brings abundance, as in 2 Chronicles 31:10 and Malachi 3:10.", :sources ["Exodus 36:7" "Ezra 5:11" "2 Chronicles 24:13" "2 Chronicles 31:10" "Malachi 3:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "דים", :translit "dayim", :gloss "sufficient"} {:he "היתה דים", :translit "haytah dayim", :gloss "was sufficient"} {:he "הלא יגנבו דים", :translit "ha-lo yignovu dayim", :gloss "will they not steal enough"} {:he "אם גנבים בלילה השחיתו דים", :translit "im ganim ba-lailah hishchitu dayim", :gloss "if thieves at night ruined enough"} {:he "במפלת אדום", :translit "be-miflat edom", :gloss "in the overthrow of Edom"} {:he "באדום נא' המה", :translit "be-edom ne-emar hemmah", :gloss "in Edom it is said 'they'"} {:he "בנו ואני אהרוס", :translit "banu ve-ani eharos", :gloss "they built up and I will tear down"} {:he "ביעקב", :translit "be-ya'akov", :gloss "in Jacob"} {:he "והמלאכה היתה דים", :translit "ve-hamelakhah haytah dayim", :gloss "and the work was sufficient"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["היתה דים" "הלא יגנבו דים" "אם גנבים בלילה השחיתו דים"]} {:kind :other, :note "Contrastive reading: Edom's work is described as 'they built' (third person, external), while Jacob's work is described as 'the work was sufficient' (divine blessing rests on it)"}], :frame {:text "The three occurrences of 'דים' in the Masorah include Genesis 31:39 ('הלא יגנבו דים'), Jeremiah 49:9 ('אם גנבים בלילה השחיתו דים'), and Exodus 36:7. The prophecy against Edom in Jeremiah 49 uses this word in the context of Edom's destruction ('they shall build and I will tear down,' Jeremiah 49:10, echoed in Obadiah 1:4), while in Jacob's context the same word denotes sufficiency and divine blessing. The contrast between Edom and Jacob draws from the oracles in Jeremiah 49 and Obadiah, where Edom faces ruin despite building.", :sources ["Genesis 31:39" "Jeremiah 49:9" "Jeremiah 49:10" "Obadiah 1:4" "Exodus 36:7"]}} {:quotes [{:he "והותר", :translit "ve-hutar", :gloss "and it was left over"} {:he "כי כה אמר ה' אכול והותר", :translit "ki koh amar Hashem ekhol ve-hutar", :gloss "for thus said the Lord eat and be left over"} {:he "מהחל התרומה להביא בית ה' אכול ושבוע והותר", :translit "me-hechel ha-terumah le-haviyt beit Hashem ekhol ve-sove'a ve-hutar", :gloss "from the beginning of the offering to bring to the house of the Lord eat and be sated and be left over"} {:he "בדה\"ב", :translit "be-divrei ha-yamim bet", :gloss "in Chronicles II"} {:he "בזכות התורה ובהמ\"ק", :translit "be-zekhut ha-torah u-vi-temple", :gloss "in the merit of Torah and the Temple"} {:he "בא שובע לעולם", :translit "ba sova la-olam", :gloss "comes satiety to the world"} {:he "למן היום אשר יוסד היכל ה'", :translit "le-min ha-yom asher yusad heikhal Hashem", :gloss "from the day that the temple of the Lord was founded"} {:he "עד מן היום הזה אברך", :translit "ad min ha-yom ha-zeh evarekh", :gloss "until this day I have blessed"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["והותר" "אכול והותר" "אכול ושבוע והותר"]} {:kind :other, :note "Links the leftover from Tabernacle offerings to leftovers from prophetic and Temple-era blessings, establishing a typology of divine satiety through sacred space"}], :frame {:text "The three occurrences of 'והותר' include Exodus 36:7, 2 Kings 4:7 ('כי כה אמר ה' אכול והותר'), and 2 Chronicles 31:10 ('אכול ושבוע והותר'). The verse 2 Chronicles 31:10 describes how from the time the Temple offerings began, there was eating, satiation, and abundance — 'כי יברך ה' את עמו'. The closing proof-text 'למן היום אשר יוסד היכל ה' וגו' עד מן היום הזה אברך' is 2 Chronicles 31:10, where the blessing of the Temple produces ongoing abundance.", :sources ["Exodus 36:7" "2 Kings 4:7" "2 Chronicles 31:10"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["והמלאכה היתה דים" "והמלאכה לא ליום אחד ולשנים" "והמלאכה גדולה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "והמלאכה היתה דים", :refs ["Exodus 36:7"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "והמלאכה לא ליום אחד ולשנים", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "והמלאכה גדולה", :refs ["1 Chronicles 29:1"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "The three occurrences span Exodus, Ezra, and Chronicles, all in contexts of building the House of God", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["היתה דים" "הלא יגנבו דים" "אם גנבים בלילה השחיתו דים"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "היתה דים", :refs ["Exodus 3